# ...but frontend/tests/build/ is SOURCE, not output.
#
# `build/` above matches a directory of that name at ANY depth, so it also
# swallowed frontend/tests/build/ — the suite that checks the SHIPPED artefact:
# that no page of either half references Google's font hosts, that the
# self-hosted faces are really bundled and really referenced, and that no inline
# <script> survives into the site. Those tests were on disk, passing locally,
# and had never once run in CI, because `actions/checkout` never received them.
#
# A guard that only runs on the author's machine is not a guard. Re-include the
# directory itself, which is what makes the files inside reachable again — git
# cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded.
!frontend/tests/build/

# Test documents with personal identity data
DUBROVNIK.pdf
ITALIE 2 PIECES IDENTITE - copie.pdf

backend/bench_result_*.json

# Development-only tools & local artifacts
backend/bench_ocr.py
.coverage
htmlcov/
.ruff_cache/

# Keys, certificates and database dumps — nothing in this repository is ever
# supposed to hold one, and that is exactly why the patterns belong here.
#
# These are not hypothetical: ops/backup.sh writes an age-encrypted PostgreSQL
# dump, ops/README.md tells you to create its private key with `age-keygen`,
# ops/restore-test.sh restores a dump locally, .github/workflows/deploy.yml
# authenticates with an SSH key, and deploy/nginx-travelapp.conf is served by a
# certbot certificate. Every one of those artefacts has a natural way of landing
# in a working directory by accident.
#
# A leaked key is not fixable by a revert — the file stays in the history and
# the only real remedy is rotating the key. A pattern is cheaper.
#
# Verified when added: none of these matches any tracked or on-disk file.
*.age
*.pem
*.crt
*.cer
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
scanid-backup.key

# PostgreSQL dumps — real passport data, the moment they are real.
*.dump
*.sql
*.sql.gz

# Local visual-comparison files (site.html, site1.html, ...): each embeds one
# revision of the public site to open side by side with Live Server. They are
# generated from folders the repository already tracks, so committing them only
# duplicates ~700 KB of pages per file. Anchored to the root with a leading
# slash, so no page of the site itself (frontend/scanid-site-v5-deploy/, ...)
# can ever match.
/site.html
/site[0-9]*.html
